  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 1258 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nMoisture continues to advect into the area, bringing scattered\nshowers this afternoon and evening. The highest chance for\nprecipitation today will be in the higher terrain, specifically \nPark County and surrounding areas. We are starting to see some \nhigh based showers and virga try and move off o the higher \nterrain and into the plains. Right now, the rain is struggling to \nreach the ground, but as we get further into the afternoon and \nevening we could see some light rain make it to the surface across\nthe urban corridor and plains. In the mountains, snow levels \nremain around 12,500ft to 13,000ft today. \n\nDrier air will briefly move through the flow aloft on Friday as \ndry air wraps around the trough. Precip chances will decrease and \ntemperatures will be well above normal Friday afternoon. Winds \nwill increase at upper levels, putting 700mb winds around 25kts to\n40kts across our southeastern counties. Mostly clear skies and \nmostly unidirectional winds in the column could allow for winds \nto occasionally mix down in the afternoon, creating gusts around \n25 to 40mph along and south of I-76.\n\nA cold front will move into our northern counties late Friday, \nprogressing through the area overnight. By mid Saturday morning, \nmodels have the front sitting just to the south and east of our \nCWA. Winds behind the front will be breezy from the \nnorth/northeast and winds ahead of the front will be breezy from \nthe south, leading to strong surface convergence. This combined \nwith lift ahead of the upper trough will allow for showers and \nstorms Saturday afternoon. The front will act as the initial \ntrigger for convection, with a line of showers and storms \ndeveloping along it. As we go into the late afternoon and evening \nhowever, coverage should start to expand further north covering \nmore of our area. Precip chances will continue into Sunday as QG \nlift ahead of the trough continues. The highest rain chances and \nrain amounts this weekend will be wherever that front sets up for\nSaturday (which right now looks to be south of our area). Amounts\nwill decrease the further north we go from the front. Snow levels\nwill drop closer to 9,000ft as the cooler temps move in this \nweekend. Scattered light rain and snow showers are expected \nthroughout the weekend and into Monday for the higher terrain. \nSnow amounts will generally remain on the lower end (<2 inches), \nwith the potential for some higher amounts in the Mosquito Range \narea. \n\nModels are hinting at a reenforcement of the cold air on Monday \nas surface high pressure builds to our northeast. This could keep\ntemperatures below normal again for Monday, before we start to \nsee some warmer temps later in the week . The progression of the \nupper trough varies between models. The Euro is a bit slower \ntracking east, which keeps upper forcing around for a bit longer, \nextending rain chances into Monday. However, we could also see a \nfaster progression of the trough, leading to rain chances ending \nlate Sunday. Weak upper level ridging and zonal flow aloft will \nbuild in behind the trough, leading to drier and slightly warmer \nconditions for Tuesday and Wednesday.",
